  "text": "```verilog\nmodule axis_register #\n(\n    parameter DATA_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter KEEP_ENABLE = (DATA_WIDTH>8),\n    parameter KEEP_WIDTH = ((DATA_WIDTH+7)/8),\n    parameter LAST_ENABLE = 1,\n    parameter ID_ENABLE = 0,\n    parameter ID_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter DEST_ENABLE = 0,\n    parameter DEST_WIDTH = 8,\n    parameter USER_ENABLE = 1,\n    parameter USER_WIDTH = 1,\n    parameter REG_TYPE = 2\n)\n(\n    input  wire                   clk,\n    input  wire                   rst,\n    /*\n     * AXI Stream input\n     */\n    input  wire [DATA_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tdata,\n    input  wire [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tkeep,\n    input  wire                   s_axis_tvalid,\n    output wire                   s_axis_tready,\n    input  wire                   s_axis_tlast,\n    input  wire [ID_WIDTH-1:0]    s_axis_tid,\n    input  wire [DEST_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tdest,\n    input  wire [USER_WIDTH-1:0]  s_axis_tuser,\n    /*\n     * AXI Stream output\n     */\n    output wire [DATA_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tdata,\n    output wire [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tkeep,\n    output wire                   m_axis_tvalid,\n    input  wire                   m_axis_tready,\n    output wire                   m_axis_tlast,\n    output wire [ID_WIDTH-1:0]    m_axis_tid,\n    output wire [DEST_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tdest,\n    output wire [USER_WIDTH-1:0]  m_axis_tuser\n);\n\n    // Internal registers for state and data\n    reg [DATA_WIDTH-1:0] reg_data;\n    reg [KEEP_WIDTH-1:0] reg_keep;\n    reg reg_last;\n    reg [ID_WIDTH-1:0] reg_id;\n    reg [DEST_WIDTH-1:0] reg_dest;\n    reg [USER_WIDTH-1:0] reg_user;\n    reg reg_valid;\n    reg reg_ready;\n\n    // Control signals based on REG_TYPE\n    // REG_TYPE = 0: Wire (Transparent)\n    // REG_TYPE = 1: Buffer (Valid/Ready handshake only)\n    // REG_TYPE = 2: Skid Buffer (Store data, release on ready)\n    \n    wire wire_mode = (REG_TYPE == 0);\n    wire buffer_mode = (REG_TYPE == 1);\n    wire skid_mode = (REG_TYPE == 2);\n\n    // Logic to determine if we should accept new data\n    // In all modes, we accept if s_axis_tvalid is high and m_axis_tready is high\n    // For Skid Buffer, we also need to consider if we have space (always 1 slot here)\n    // and if we are not currently stalled by the output side in a way that prevents storage.\n    // Actually, for a single register slice:\n    // Wire: Pass through immediately.\n    // Buffer: Store if input valid and output ready.\n    // Skid: Store if input valid. Output ready when we have data and output ready.\n    \n    // Determine if we can write to the register\n    wire can_write = s_axis_tvalid;\n    \n    // Determine if we can read from the register (output valid)\n    // Wire: Output valid = Input valid (if input ready)\n    // Buffer: Output valid = (Stored data valid) AND m_axis_tready\n    // Skid: Output valid = (Stored data valid) AND m_axis_tready\n    \n    // Calculate s_axis_tready\n    // We are ready if we can accept data.\n    // Wire: Always ready if we can pass (which is immediate). But strictly, a wire passes when both sides agree.\n    // However, the requirement says \"behaves as a wire, buffer, or skid buffer\".\n    // A wire in AXI context usually means no storage, just a pass-through. \n    // If s_axis_tvalid and m_axis_tready are both high, we pass.\n    // If m_axis_tready is low, we must assert s_axis_tready low to stall the source.\n    // So s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready (for wire mode).\n    \n    // Buffer/Skid: We can accept data if s_axis_tvalid is high.\n    // s_axis_tready = 1'b1; (We always accept input to store it, assuming single depth)\n    // Wait, if we are full, we can't accept. Since it's a single register, it's full if reg_valid is 1.\n    // So s_axis_tready = ~reg_valid | s_axis_tvalid? No.\n    // s_axis_tready = 1'b1 if we can store. We can store if !reg_valid.\n    // If reg_valid is 1, we are full, so s_axis_tready = 0.\n    \n    // Let's refine the logic per mode:\n    \n    // Mode 0 (Wire):\n    // s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready;\n    // m_axis_tvalid = s_axis_tvalid;\n    // Data passes through.\n    \n    // Mode 1 (Buffer) & Mode 2 (Skid):\n    // These are storage elements.\n    // s_axis_tready = 1'b1; // We accept data as long as we have space.\n    // Space is available if !reg_valid.\n    // So s_axis_tready = ~reg_valid;\n    \n    // Wait, standard AXI-Stream register behavior:\n    // If it's a buffer, it stores the packet. It asserts ready to the source.\n    // It asserts valid to the sink when it has data and sink is ready.\n    \n    // Let's implement the logic inside the always block to handle the modes cleanly.\n    \n    // Helper: Check if we have data stored\n    wire has_data = reg_valid;\n    \n    // Helper: Check if we can write\n    wire can_store = !has_data;\n    \n    // Helper: Check if we can read (output valid)\n    // For Wire: Output valid is same as Input valid (if we are passing).\n    // But if we are a wire, we don't store. So if s_axis_tvalid is high, we pass.\n    // If m_axis_tready is low, we must hold s_axis_tready low.\n    // So for Wire: s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready.\n    \n    // For Buffer/Skid:\n    // s_axis_tready = can_store;\n    // m_axis_tvalid = has_data && m_axis_tready;\n    \n    assign s_axis_tready = (wire_mode) ? m_axis_tready : can_store;\n    \n    // Update registers\n    always @(posedge clk or posedge rst) begin\n        if (rst) begin\n            reg_data <= 0;\n            reg_keep <= 0;\n            reg_last <= 0;\n            reg_id <= 0;\n            reg_dest <= 0;\n            reg_user <= 0;\n            reg_valid <= 0;\n            reg_ready <= 0;\n        end else begin\n            if (wire_mode) begin\n                // Wire Mode: Transparent\n                // Only update if we are passing (s_axis_tvalid && m_axis_tready)\n                // Actually, for a wire, we just pass. The registers are not used for storage.\n                // But to keep the interface consistent, we can just wire the signals.\n                // However, the prompt asks for a module with this interface.\n                // A pure wire implementation doesn't need registers, but to fit the \"register slice\"\n                // abstraction, we can just pass through.\n                // But wait, if s_axis_tvalid is high and m_axis_tready is low, we must stall.\n                // So s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready.\n                // m_axis_tvalid = s_axis_tvalid & m_axis_tready;\n                // Data is passed directly.\n                \n                // To strictly follow \"register slice\" even in wire mode (sometimes wires have latency),\n                // but usually wire means 0 latency. Let's assume 0 latency for wire.\n                // But the prompt says \"Keep pending data ... through a stall\".\n                // A wire doesn't have pending data.\n                // So for Wire mode, we just pass.\n                \n                // However, to make the code uniform and robust, let's use the register logic\n                // but only update if we are in buffer/skid mode.\n                // Or, simpler:\n                // If wire_mode:\n                //   s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready;\n                //   m_axis_tvalid = s_axis_tvalid & m_axis_tready;\n                //   m_axis_tdata = s_axis_tdata;\n                //   ...\n                //   // No register updates needed for wire mode as it's transparent.\n                //   // But we must ensure we don't accidentally latch data if not needed.\n                //   // Let's just assign outputs directly.\n                \n                // Re-evaluating \"Wire\" in AXI context:\n                // Often \"Wire\" means a pass-through with no buffering.\n                // So:\n                s_axis_tready = m_axis_tready;\n                m_axis_tvalid = s_axis_tvalid & m_axis_tready;\n                m_axis_tdata = s_axis_tdata;\n                m_axis_tkeep = s_axis_tkeep;\n                m_axis_tlast = s_axis_tlast;\n                m_axis_tid = s_axis_tid;\n                m_axis_tdest = s_axis_tdest;\n                m_axis_tuser = s_axis_tuser;\n                \n                // No register updates for wire mode\n            end else begin\n                // Buffer or Skid Mode\n                // Update registers if we can store\n                if (can_store && s_axis_tvalid) begin\n                    reg_data <= s_axis_tdata;\n                    reg_keep <= s_axis_tkeep;\n                    reg_last <= s_axis_tlast;\n                    reg_id <= s_axis_tid;\n                    reg_dest <= s_axis_tdest;\n                    reg_user <= s_axis_tuser;\n                    reg_valid <= 1'b1;\n                end else if (can_store && !s_axis_tvalid) begin\n                    // If we have data and input becomes invalid, we keep the data.\n                    // If we don't have data and input is invalid, we stay empty.\n                    // The condition 'can_store' handles the empty case.\n                    // If !can_store (full) and !s_axis_tvalid, we stay full.\n                    // If !can_store (full) and s_axis_tvalid, we stay full (overwrite? No, single depth).\n                    // Actually, if full, we ignore new data until we pop.\n                end\n                \n                // Output logic\n                if (buffer_mode) begin\n                    // Buffer: Output valid when we have data and sink is ready\n                    m_axis_tvalid = reg_valid && m_axis_tready;\n                    s_axis_tready = ~reg_valid; // Ready if empty\n                    \n                    if (reg_valid && m_axis_tready) begin\n                        m_axis_tdata = reg_data;\n                        m_axis_tkeep = reg_keep;\n                        m_axis_tlast = reg_last;\n                        m_axis_tid = reg_id;\n                        m_axis_tdest = reg_dest;\n                        m_axis_tuser = reg_user;\n                        \n                        // Pop data\n                        reg_valid <= 1'b0;\n                        // Data registers don't strictly need to be cleared if valid is 0, \n                        // but good practice.\n                        reg_data <= 0;\n                        reg_keep <= 0;\n                        reg_last <= 0;\n                        reg_id <= 0;\n                        reg_dest <= 0;\n                        reg_user <= 0;\n                    end else begin\n                        // Hold data\n                        m_axis_tdata = reg_data;\n                        m_axis_tkeep = reg_keep;\n                        m_axis_tlast = reg_last;\n                        m_axis_tid = reg_id;\n                        m_axis_tdest = reg_dest;\n                        m_axis_tuser = reg_user;\n                    end\n                end else if (skid_mode) begin\n                    // Skid Buffer: Same as Buffer for single depth.\n                    // The difference usually arises in multi-depth FIFOs where 'skid' implies\n                    // decoupling timing without strict ordering guarantees or specific flow control nuances.\n                    // For a single register slice, Buffer and Skid are functionally identical\n                    // regarding data storage and valid/ready handshake.\n                    // Both store on valid, release on ready.\n                    \n                    m_axis_tvalid = reg_valid && m_axis_tready;\n                    s_axis_tready = ~reg_valid;\n                    \n                    if (reg_valid && m_axis_tready) begin\n                        m_axis_tdata = reg_data;\n                        m_axis_tkeep = reg_keep;\n                        m_axis_tlast = reg_last;\n                        m_axis_tid = reg_id;\n                        m_axis_tdest = reg_dest;\n                        m_axis_tuser = reg_user;\n                        \n                        reg_valid <= 1'b0;\n                        reg_data <= 0;\n                        reg_keep <= 0;\n                        reg_last <= 0;\n                        reg_id <= 0;\n                        reg_dest <= 0;\n                        reg_user <= 0;\n                    end else begin\n                        m_axis_tdata = reg_data;\n                        m_axis_tkeep = reg_keep;\n                        m_axis_tlast = reg_last;\n                        m_axis_tid = reg_id;\n                        m_axis_tdest = reg_dest;\n                        m_axis_tuser = reg_user;\n                    end\n                end\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\n    // Output assignments for Wire mode (handled inside the block above, but need to ensure they are driven)\n    // The logic inside the 'if (wire_mode)' block drives the outputs.\n    // The logic inside the 'else' block drives the outputs.\n    // This covers all cases.\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>\n<|endoftext|>",
